= PKK is founded

{!<img src="img/kurdish/pkk.png" alt="PKK is founded" style="display:block;max-width:100%;max-height:300px;width:auto;height:auto;margin:12px auto;object-fit:contain">!}

Based in Ankara University, a group of student activists, guerilla fighters and political theorists have held the first congress of what they have named ''Partiya Karkerên Kurdistanê'' (Kurdistan Workers Party, PKK for short). The newly-established organisation has adopted a ''people's war'' strategy against the Turkish state along with Marxist-Leninist ideological teachings and have vowed to establish an independent socialist republic in Turkish Kurdistan.

During the 1960s the Kurdish political movement was largely monopolised by the Türkiye Kürdistan Demokratik Partisi (Kurdistan Democratic Party of Turkey, T-KDP for short). T-KDP was organised along conservative, centre-right Barzanist lines and maintained strong ties to the Barzani-controlled Partiya Demokratîk a Kurdistanê (Kurdistan Democratic Party, PDK for short) and followed the line of gaining concessions regarding local autonomy, language and political participation from Turkish authorities through nonviolent societal mobilisation. However, the party began to split as a left-wing, militant wing led by Sait Kırmızıtoprak (known more commonly by his nom de guerre Dr. Şivan) chafed against the party's conservative leadership, demanding a switch to armed action.

The PKK presents a growing strain of left-wing to far-left organisations on the spectrum of the Kurdish nationalist movement. Owing it's roots to a student-led faction called ''Apocular'' (literally ''Apoists'', named after the moniker of their leader Abdullah Öcalan, alternatively called Kürdistan Devrimciler.

